      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the distance between the foci of the given ellipse, we need to understand its standard form and relevant properties.\n\nThe standard form of an ellipse centered at the origin is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac {x^2}{a^2} + \\frac {y^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nFor an ellipse, the distance \\( c \\) from the center to each focus is given by the relationship:\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the two foci of the ellipse is twice the distance \\( c \\) to each focus from the center.\n\nGiven in the problem:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Ellipse centered at the origin} \\quad \\frac{x^2}{25} + \\frac{y^2}{16} = 1\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\n\\[\na^2 = 25 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad a = 5\n\\]\n\\[\nb^2 = 16 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad b = 4\n\\]\n\nUsing the formula \\( c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2} \\):\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{25 - 16} = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the two foci is then:\n\n\\[\n2c = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the foci of the ellipse is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{6}\\)",
